 if v ∈ ˙H θ is a\\nsolution of (5.4), then v ∈ ˙H min(θ,s−1)+1. Hence, it is suﬃcient to consider solutions v in\\n˙H σ, for σ >1/2.\\nIn terms of the Fourier coeﬃcients (5.4) reads\\nvk −\\n∑\\nk1+k2=k\\nei3kk1k2tϕk1vk2\\nk1k2\\n= 0, k ∈ Z0.\\nSetting uk =e−ik3tvk and ψk =e−ik3tϕk and taking into account (3.7) we obtain\\nuk −\\n∑\\nk1+k2=k\\nψk1uk2\\nk1k2\\n= 0 (5.5)\\n\\nREGULARIZATION MECHANISM FOR THE PERIODIC KDV EQUATION 17\\nwith ∥u∥ ˙H s = ∥v∥ ˙H s. We now deﬁne w(x) and ξ(x) by setting\\nwk = uk\\nik, ξ k = ψk\\nik, k ∈ Z0, w 0 =ξ0 = 0.\\nBy deﬁnition, both w and ξ have mean value zero:\\n∫ 2π\\n0 w(x)dx =\\n∫ 2π\\n0 ξ(x)dx = 0 and,\\nin addition, w′(x) = u(x), ξ′(x) = ψ(x) so that ∥u∥ ˙H s = ∥w∥ ˙H s+1, ∥ψ∥ ˙H s = ∥ξ∥ ˙H s+1.\\nMultiplying (5.5) by eikx and summing with respect to all k ∈ Z we see that\\nw(x) =\\n∑\\nk∈Z0\\nuk\\nik eikx, w ′(x) = u(x),\\nsatisﬁes the following boundary value problem:\\nw′(x) +ξ(x)w(x) = 1\\n2π\\n∫ 2π\\n0\\nξ(x)w(x)dx,\\nw is periodic, with period 2 π, and\\n∫ 2π\\n0\\nw(x)dx = 0,\\n(5.6)\\nin the classical sense since it is given that ξ ∈H s+1 ⊂C 1, for s> 1/2, and we are looking\\nfor a solution w ∈H θ+1 ⊂ C 1, for θ >1/2. Note that the right-hand side of (5.6) is the\\nprojection Π 0(ξw). Then\\nw′…5846 tokens truncated…k3\\n˜z2\\nk1+k2+k3\\n) 1/2(∑\\nk1\\n∑\\nk3\\n|wk3|2|k3|2s|vk1|2\\n) 1/2\\n≤\\nc3ns∥v∥ ˙H 0∥w∥ ˙H 0∥z∥ ˙H −s + 2c3∥v∥ ˙H 0∥w∥ ˙H s∥z∥ ˙H −s,\\nwhere c3 ≤π/\\n√\\n3. Hence, after a ﬁnite\", \"next_offset\": 64000, \"has_more\": true, \"evidence\": [{\"path\": \"external-sources/access-56aa1f7cf7b94bbbbd25b929c0efa9ed.json\", \"sha256\": \"bc55efe20c1bab6905ea7cfbb42e57e0337e82e9b5c2d2c118933983392c5a7f\"}, {\"path\": \"external-sources/access-56aa1f7cf7b94bbbbd25b929c0efa9ed.pdf\", \"sha256\": \"b6958c0b155bb8463f85900c4beaf75b1cf399382101a720cc39edf82cfac52e\"}, {\"path\": \"external-sources/access-56aa1f7cf7b94bbbbd25b929c0efa9ed.txt\", \"sha256\": \"3a37d646c752a691c70b1b58bcd3525e413905f83cb9f3e988c1