para("S001",`H001–H003 give T>0, 0<gamma<=1 and R>0. Fix f0 under L1. Reflection and sign preserve each Fourier magnitude: the source initial datum -f0(-x) has coefficient -hat f0(-k). For a mean-zero datum and s>=0, ||f||_(dot H^s)<=||f||_(H^s)<=2^(s/2)||f||_(dot H^s), since |k|<=<k><=sqrt(2)|k| at k!=0. Apply E001 with s=gamma, T_s=T, B0=R and initial homogeneous norm bounded by R. The source Galerkin bound passes to its limiting flow by Theorems 4.2–4.3 there, and the source uniqueness/continuity theorem is 5.1 if gamma>1/2 and 6.3 otherwise. Thus obtain U with a uniform homogeneous bound depending only on T,gamma,R.`,["H001","H002","H003","E001"],[],["T","gamma","R","Ttor","Fourier","H","f0","U"],[fact("F001","The source flow U exists, is unique and continuous in dot H^gamma, and has a bound depending only on T,gamma,R.",["U","T","gamma","R"],["L1"])],["L1"]),
para("S002",`Define u(t,x)=-U(t,-x). For smooth functions u_t=-U_t, u_x=U_x and u_xxx=U_xxx at (t,-x), so u_t+u_xxx=-U U_x=u u_x=(1/2)partial_x(u^2). The same change of variables in test functions proves the distributional identity for the limiting solution. The inverse reflection gives uniqueness. Sobolev norm comparison from S001 yields a common bound A(T,gamma,R); limits obey it since each finite Fourier norm sum passes to the limit and the full positive norm sum is the supremum of its finite sums. Continuity in H^gamma extends an almost-everywhere bound to every time. Smooth data are in every dot H^s; apply E001 at each positive integer s and identify the flows by uniqueness, giving smoothness sufficient for all later calculations.`,["E001"],["F001"],["T","gamma","R","Ttor","Fourier","H","KdV","U","u","f0"],[fact("F002","For f0 under L1, u is the unique exact target-sign solution and sup ||u||_(H^gamma)<=A(T,gamma,R); smooth f0 gives a smooth solution.",["u","f0","T","gamma","R","H","KdV"],["L1"])]),
para("S003",`S_t is a Fourier multiplier of modulus one, hence preserves every H^s norm, reality and zero mean. Therefore the exact twist v(t)=S_(-t)u(t) has M=1+sup||u||_(H^gamma)<=1+A(T,gamma,R). Discharge L1 and quantify over all f0 in the stated ball.`,[],["F002"],["S","u","f0","H","T","gamma","R"],[],[],["L1"]),
para("S004",`For arbitrary B>0 and two real mean-zero L2 initial data with norm sum <=B, apply E001 Theorem 6.4 and its Theorem 6.5 at theta=0, T_s=T. The source twist is unitary, and the same reflection identifies the physical target-sign flows; their normalized L2 distance is unchanged. Thus sup_[0,T]||u-tilde u||_2<=C(T,B)||u0-tilde u0||_2. This includes convergence from Fourier polynomial data, which tend to f0 in L2 by the defining square-summable Fourier norm. The limit agrees with the H^gamma flow by uniqueness, establishing the smooth-approximation convention.`,["E001"],[],["T","B","L2","S","Fourier","KdV"])
