para("S001",`Begin with finite support. Decompose each sequence into shells 2^j<=|k|<2^(j+1), j>=0, with shell norms X_(r,j). Fix one of the at most 24 orderings of the four shell indices, and relabel slots within this calculation so j1>=j2>=j3>=j4. A nonzero zero-sum interaction has j1-j2<=2: otherwise the largest magnitude is at least 2^(j2+3), exceeding three times the upper magnitude 2^(j2+1) of the remaining entries. For fixed values of the two smaller frequencies, the sum of the two large-frequency sequences constrained by the zero sum is <=X_(1,j1)X_(2,j2) by Cauchy–Schwarz (translated reflection preserves the ell2 norm). A shell has 2^(j+1) integer entries, so summing the two small sequences in ell1 gives <=C*2^((j3+j4)/2)*product_r X_(r,jr). Dividing by Third>=2^j3 leaves C*2^(-(j3-j4)/2).`,[],[],["Third","L2","x1","x2","x3","x4","Xshell"],[fact("F001","For each ordered shell interaction j1>=j2>=j3>=j4 with j1-j2 in {0,1,2}, its weighted contribution is <=C 2^(-(j3-j4)/2) product_r X_(r,jr).",["Third","Xshell"])]),
para("S002",`For each q=j1-j2 in {0,1,2}, enlarge the nonnegative sum by dropping the upper restriction on the smaller indices. Its bound factors as C*(sum_J X_(1,J)X_(2,J-q))*(sum_(ell>=m)2^(-(ell-m)/2)X_(3,ell)X_(4,m)). The first factor is <=||X_1||_ell2||X_2||_ell2. Write r=ell-m in the second and apply Cauchy–Schwarz for each fixed r; it is <=sum_(r>=0)2^(-r/2)||X_3||_ell2||X_4||_ell2=(1-2^(-1/2))^(-1)||X_3||_ell2||X_4||_ell2. The shell norm squares sum to the original norm square. Summing over the orderings and three offsets proves the finite-support estimate.`,[],["F001"],["L2","Third","x1","x2","x3","x4","Xshell"],[fact("F002","The asserted four-linear estimate holds for all nonnegative finite-support sequences with one absolute support-independent constant.",["Third","x1","x2","x3","x4"])]),
para("S003",`For arbitrary nonnegative ell2 sequences restrict each to |k|<=m. Their norms are no larger than the full norms. Every finite set of interaction terms occurs in some such truncation; hence the infinite positive sum is the supremum of these finite sums and has the same bound. Restricting to a subset only decreases a nonnegative sum. This proves both assertions without an exchange of conditionally convergent sums.`,[],["F002"],["L2","Third","x1","x2","x3","x4"])
]);
node("N004",`Remainder has a unique continuous trilinear extension from mean-zero H^sigma inputs to L2 for each 0<=sigma<=1, and ||Remainder_(t0,tau)(f1,f2,f3)||_2<=C_sigma*tau^(1+sigma)*product_j||fj||_(H^sigma), uniformly for all t0 real, tau>0 and Fourier support. The same estimate holds with any extra frequency multiplier of modulus <=1, including restrictions Phi=0 or Phi!=0. In particular if mean-zero w in H^gamma, mean-zero f in L2, ||w||_(H^gamma)<=M0 and delta=||f-w||_2<=1, then for 0<tau<=1, ||Remainder_(t0,tau)(f,f,f)||_2<=C_(M0,gamma)*tau*(tau^gamma+delta).`,["D003","D004","D013","D014","D015"],["sigma","t0","tau","f1","f2","f3","w","f","M0","delta"].map(x=>b(x,["f1","f2","f3","w","f"].includes(x)?"mean-zero periodic function":"real parameter as quantified")),["gamma","H","L2","eta","Remainder","Third","sigma","t0","tau","f1","f2","f3","w","f","M0","delta"],"problem.md:253–299.");
