para("S001",`Assume L1: 0<=sigma<=1, tau>0 and t0 real. Only nonzero a,b,c,k and p=a+b!=0 need be considered in the Fourier sum. Put d=-k, A=|ab| and B=|cd|; |alpha/beta|=A/B. N002 gives |eta|/|k|<=6/Third. Its second covariance bound gives |eta|/(tau|abck|)<=3|p|*min(A,B)/(AB)=3|p|/max(A,B)<=18/Third. The geometric mean with weights 1-sigma,sigma is |eta|/(|k| |abc|^sigma)<=6^(1-sigma)*18^sigma*tau^sigma/Third. This includes sigma=0,1. Zero input frequencies give eta=0 and the output k=0 was defined to vanish.`,["N002"],[],["eta","Remainder","Third","sigma","tau","t0"],[fact("F001","For the nonzero interactions, |eta|/(|k||abc|^sigma)<=6^(1-sigma)18^sigma tau^sigma/Third(a,b,c,-k).",["eta","Third","sigma","tau"],["L1"])],["L1"]),
para("S002",`For polynomial f1,f2,f3 in H^sigma, dualize the Fourier formula against any finite ell2 sequence. Set x1(a)=|a|^sigma|hat f1(a)|, x2 and x3 analogously, and x4(d) equal to the absolute dual coefficient at -d. Taking absolute values removes exp(-i*t0*Phi), whose modulus is one. F001 and N003 bound the pairing by C_sigma*tau^(1+sigma)*product_j||fj||_(H^sigma)*||dual||_ell2, including the factor 1/18. Ell2 duality proves the stated norm bound. A multiplier of modulus <=1 only decreases this absolute envelope, so the estimate includes arbitrary restrictions.`,["N003"],["F001"],["Remainder","Third","Fourier","H","L2","sigma","tau","t0","f1","f2","f3"],[fact("F002","The claimed trilinear bound, with any modulus-one majorized multiplier, holds for polynomial inputs.",["Remainder","H","sigma","tau","t0","f1","f2","f3"],["L1"])]),
para("S003",`Polynomials are dense in H^sigma by its definition. The trilinear estimate bounds differences by a sum of three terms, each with one difference input and two bounded inputs. Thus polynomial approximations produce a Cauchy sequence in complete L2 with a unique limit independent of approximations; this defines the continuous extension and retains the estimate. Discharge L1 and quantify over all parameters and inputs.`,[],["F002"],["Remainder","H","L2","sigma","tau","t0","f1","f2","f3"],[],[],["L1"]),
para("S004",`H002 gives 0<gamma<=1. Under the final claim's conditions use the exponent-gamma bound on (w,w,w). For the difference use the telescoping trilinear expansion R(f,f,f)-R(w,w,w)=R(f-w,f,f)+R(w,f-w,f)+R(w,w,f-w), each at the same t0,tau, and the exponent-zero bound. Its norm is <=C*tau*delta*(||f||_2+||w||_2)^2. Since ||f||_2<=M0+1, this is <=C_M0*tau*delta; the pure w term is <=C_(M0,gamma)*tau^(1+gamma). For 0<tau<=1 this proves the required numerical-state estimate without a numerical H^gamma bound.`,["H002"],[],["gamma","H","L2","Remainder","w","f","M0","delta"])
]);
