s("S001",`All claims in this paragraph are universally quantified over the indicated functions and real times. S_t has multiplier of modulus one, conjugate-compatible at k and -k, and does not mix frequencies, proving the isometry, reality, mean and projection assertions. For mean-zero f, Cauchy–Schwarz gives ||Dinv f||_infinity<= (sum_(k!=0)k^(-2))^(1/2)||f||_2 and ||Dinv f||_(H^1)<=sqrt(2)||f||_2. For arbitrary A in H^1 its Fourier ell1 norm is bounded by (sum_k<k>^(-2))^(1/2)||A||_(H^1), giving H^1->L^infinity. The product rule and this embedding give ||AB||_(H^1)<=C||A||_(H^1)||B||_(H^1). Apply this to the two inverse derivatives in Jop for its H^1 bound. Bounding Dinv B in L^infinity and Dinv A in L2 gives the asymmetric Jop bound.`,[],[],["Fourier","H","L2","S","Dinv","Jop","Pnz"],[f("F001","All isometry, projection, inverse-derivative, H^1 algebra and Jop bounds in N005 hold.",["Fourier","H","L2","S","Dinv","Jop","Pnz"])]),
s("S002",`For any L2 F,G the coefficient of FG has absolute value at most ||F||_2||G||_2 by Cauchy–Schwarz in the convolution. Thus the squared H^(-pstar) norm of Bop_t(f,g) is <=||f||_2^2||g||_2^2 sum_(k!=0)k^2<k>^(-2*pstar), a finite sum because pstar=23/14>3/2. The same coefficient bound with sum_(k!=0)k^(-2) gives ||Dinv Pnz(FG)||_2<=C||F||_2||G||_2. For mean-zero A, <k>^(-pstar)<=|k|^(-1), proving ||A||_(H^(-pstar))<=||Dinv A||_2. Density extends all these continuous product bounds from polynomials; the product FG is an L1 function and Fourier convolution agrees with it by L2 approximation and the L1 product estimate.`,[],[],["Fourier","H","L2","Dinv","Pnz","Bop","pstar"],[f("F002","The Bop, inverse derivative of a product and negative-norm comparison in N005 hold uniformly in time.",["H","L2","Dinv","Pnz","Bop","pstar"])]),
s("S003",`For any time-smooth spatially smooth mean-zero f,g, the k!=0 coefficient of Jop_t(f,g) is sum_(a+b=k) exp(-i*t*3*k*a*b)*(i*a)^(-1)*(i*b)^(-1)*hat f(a)*hat g(b). Differentiating its phase produces 3*i*k, so partial_t Jop_t(f,g)=3 Bop_t(f,g)+Jop_t(f',g)+Jop_t(f,g'). Smooth Fourier series and all derivatives here are absolutely convergent. Integrating yields the stated normal-form identity, with both endpoints. Symmetry follows from the product. Since Dinv partial_x=Pnz, substitution gives Jop_t(Bop_t(f,g),h)=S_(-t)Pnz{Pnz[(S_t f)(S_t g)]*(S_t Dinv h)}, whose integral is Cop. For piecewise smooth functions apply on each smooth time piece and add; continuous endpoint values telescope.`,[],[],["Fourier","S","Dinv","Pnz","Bop","Jop","Cop"],[f("F003","The normal-form identity with both endpoints, symmetry, and the Jop-Bop to Cop identity hold on smooth intervals and concatenate across continuous piecewise smooth intervals.",["Bop","Jop","Cop"])]),
s("S004",`For fixed tau>0, each term in Ftau is an H^1 product of inverse derivatives, hence a continuous quadratic L2->H^1 map by F001. In Htau the first term is again an H^1 product. The bilinear mean P0(f^2) is continuous by the L2 product bound, so the second term is continuous. Its third term is an H^1 cubic product followed by Dinv, and its fourth term is a product of Dinv2 Ftau(f) and Dinv f followed by Dinv2. All group factors preserve Sobolev norms and all coefficients, including tau^(-1), are finite for fixed tau. This proves L2 continuity. Pnz and inverse derivatives force zero mean except in the second term; that term is a scalar multiple of S_tau Dinv f and also has zero mean. Each operation preserves reality; smooth functions remain smooth under all these operations. The recurrence therefore has well-defined continuous finite iterates and preserves real mean-zero data.`,[],["F001","F002"],["S","P0","Pnz","Dinv","Ftau","Htau","Psi","H","L2"])
]);
node("N006",`For every interval I=[a,b] in [0,infinity), |I|<=1, and mean-zero continuous piecewise smooth functions f1,f2,f3 with finite Xnorm(I), ||Cop_I(f1,f2,f3)||_2<=C|I| product_j||fj||_X(I)+C||fi||_(L^infinity(I;H^(-pstar))) product_(j!=i)||fj||_(L^infinity(I;L2)) for each i=1,2,3. The same estimate holds for arbitrary continuous functions in Xnorm with the regularity specified by E002, in particular with alpha_s=0. For time-independent mean-zero fj in H^sigma, 0<=sigma<=1, ||Cop_I(f1,f2,f3)||_2<=C_sigma |I|^sigma product_j||fj||_(H^sigma), interpreted with the bounded extension. Constants are uniform in interval location and Fourier support.`,["D003","D004","D018","D019","D020"],["I","f1","f2","f3","sigma"].map(x=>b(x,x==="I"?"time interval":x==="sigma"?"real":"periodic space-time function")),["H","L2","Cop","pstar","Xnorm","I","f1","f2","f3","sigma"],"problem.md:334–344,367–368; source norm bridge.");
