s("S001",`Instantiate E002 on arbitrary I=[a,b] with 0<=a<=b, b-a<=1 and take alpha_s=0 for the time-dependent estimate. Its operator formula is exactly Cop_I because exp(t partial_x^3)=S_(-t). Its derivative exponent is -pstar=-23/14. The source H^s and L2 norms, including derivative and time-supremum norms, equal sqrt(2*pi) times the corresponding normalized norms. Dividing the source inequality by sqrt(2*pi) absorbs the fixed product factor into C and gives the first asserted inequality for every chosen negative-norm slot. Continuous piecewise smooth inputs have distributional derivatives equal to their stepwise derivatives almost everywhere, without point masses, because their values agree at the breaks; hence their finite Xnorm suffices.`,["E002"],[],["S","H","L2","Cop","pstar","Xnorm"]),
s("S002",`For any 0<=sigma<=1 and three static H^sigma inputs apply E002 with alpha_s=sigma on the same arbitrary interval. The same norm conversion gives ||Cop_I||_2<=C_sigma |I|^sigma product||fj||_(H^sigma). For |I|=0 the integral vanishes, so the possible endpoint convention 0^0 causes no issue. Polynomial approximation and the trilinear bound provide the continuous extension as in the Cauchy argument: expand a difference in one slot at a time and use completeness of L2. This justifies the claimed rough static domain.`,["E002"],[],["H","L2","Cop","pstar","Xnorm"])
