s("S001",`H002 gives 0<gamma<=1. In E004 set sigma_s=gamma and at gamma=1/2 choose epsilon=1/8. Its a(sigma_s) is exactly agamma. Source norms are sqrt(2*pi) times ours; dividing the product estimate by sqrt(2*pi) changes only its constant. This proves ||fg||_2<=C_gamma||f||_(H^gamma)||g||_(H^agamma) for arbitrary f,g in the displayed spaces.`,["H002","E004"],[],["gamma","agamma","H","L2"]),
s("S002",`Take arbitrary 0<tau<=1, n>=0 and mean-zero w with ||w||_(H^gamma)<=M0. E003 with sigma_s=gamma, g1=g2=w and epsilon=1/8 in the middle case estimates our Stage after the same fixed norm conversion. At beta=agamma the exponent theta=(1+gamma-agamma)/(1+gamma-beta0) equals (1/2+2*gamma)/(5/2-gamma) for gamma<1/2, 11/17 for gamma=1/2 and (1+gamma)/2 for gamma>1/2. These are >=gamma: in the first case the numerator minus gamma times denominator is gamma^2-gamma/2+1/2>0; the middle is >1/2; the last follows from gamma<=1. In each case beta0<=agamma<=1+gamma and 0<=agamma<1. Since tau<=1, tau^theta<=tau^gamma. At beta=1+gamma the source exponent is zero. This proves both uniform stage estimates, with constants depending only on gamma,M0.`,["E003"],[],["gamma","agamma","Stage","H","L2"])
